# Component State/Display Separation

Components that manage state must be split into two distinct layers:

| Layer | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| **Display controller** | Pure rendering — receives state as props, no `useState` |
| **State controller (HOC)** | Owns state via `useState`, wraps the display controller |

## Pattern

```jsx
// Display controller — pure, stateless
export const FoldDisplay = ({ isOpen, toggle, children }) => { ... }

// State controller HOC — owns state, delegates rendering
const Fold = ({ defaultOpen = false, children }) => {
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(defaultOpen)
  const toggle = () => setIsOpen((prev) => !prev)
  return <FoldDisplay isOpen={isOpen} toggle={toggle}>{children}</FoldDisplay>
}
export default Fold
```

## Rules

- Components with **no state** do not need this separation — a single stateless component is sufficient
- The display controller is **always a named export** so it can be used standalone when external state is needed
- The state controller HOC is the **default export** and is the primary consumer-facing API
- Both must live in the same file
- The design is based around **passing children and cloning them** to allow composition in JSX — display controllers use `React.cloneElement` to curry props into children rather than rendering fixed sub-components directly

See [src/components/Fold/fold.jsx](mdc:src/components/Fold/fold.jsx) for the reference implementation.
